# Temperature input/output verification

The tests and simulator execute the same C++ temperature calculation as production
firmware. Its 26 scenarios check weather-cap boundaries, winter valve authority,
coefficient/sign behavior, room permission, mode precedence, malformed inputs,
physical maximum/half-step limits and fail-safe behavior. Negative valve-mode
forecast adjustments require a fresh, explicit all-rooms-satisfied permission.

Use one Python environment for build and serial tools:

```sh
python -m pip install platformio==6.1.19 esptool==4.11.0 pyserial
```

Python 3.11 is the verified CI/local setup. With multiple PlatformIO installations,
`python -m platformio` selects the tools belonging to that interpreter.

## Temperature calculation tests (ESP32 / COM3)

Run the isolated Unity tests on the USB-connected ESP32:

```sh
python -m platformio test -e temperature_test --upload-port COM3
```

The `temperature_test` environment compiles `src/feed_temperature.cpp` and the
Unity tests only. It does not build or initialize the controller, CAN, WiFi,
MQTT, or web server.

## Forecast lifecycle tests (ESP32 / COM3)

```sh
python -m platformio test -e forecast_test --upload-port COM3
```

The 9 cases compile the real `src/forecast.cpp` and `src/feed_temperature.cpp`.
`test/forecast_support/context.h` replaces only application CAN/network bindings;
the forecast state machine, ESP32 clock, critical sections and calculation remain
real. Assertions check disabled application, live coefficient changes, permission
transitions without a changed correction, expiry, receipt-time lifetime, invalid
sample rejection, and rejection of older queued samples against newer pending
or active samples—even with identical millisecond timestamps.

Run PlatformIO commands sequentially: changes to project configuration can
autoclean other environments, and concurrent processes can delete each other's
build artifacts. These tests replace the application on COM3, not production.

## Queued runtime-control tests (ESP32 / COM3)

```sh
python -m platformio test -e runtime_control_test --upload-port COM3
```

This environment compiles the real `src/mqtt.cpp`, `src/forecast.cpp` and
`src/feed_temperature.cpp` implementations and uses the production command,
configuration and heating data types. It disables MQTT transmission and supplies
only passive application bindings; it does not connect to WiFi/MQTT or initialize
the boiler/CAN controller. The behavior tests cover whole-document validation,
preservation/replacement of queued control and forecast input, recognized versus
unknown fields, accepted boolean forms, numeric bounds, and the resulting
ordinary feed-temperature calculation.

## Interactive temperature simulator

Build and upload the serial JSON simulator to the test ESP32:

```sh
python -m platformio run -e temperature_simulator -t upload --upload-port COM3
python scripts/temperature_console.py --all
```

Use `python scripts/temperature_console.py --interactive` to send one JSON
object per input line, or pass one request with `--json`, for example:

```sh
python scripts/temperature_console.py --json '{"ValveScaling":true,"DynamicAdaption":true,"OutsideTemperature":11.59,"ValveOpening":32}'
```

Fields use the `FeedTemperatureInputs` member names. Omitted fields use the
same defaults as the calculation API. Each request returns a JSON object with
`Setpoint` and `DisableFastHeatup`; malformed JSON, unsupported fields, and
fields with the wrong JSON type return an `error` object. The simulator has no
heater I/O or production network setup.

The simulator accepts at most 511 input bytes per line. It reconstructs the
input defaults for each request; one scenario cannot leak its settings into the
next. The `HoldMinimumTemperature` input represents the intentional no-clock
fail-safe hold; ordinary invalid/equal curve endpoints still fail off.

Raw forecast scenarios additionally accept `ForecastEnabled`,
`ForecastCoefficient`, `ForecastTemperatureChange` and `ForecastRoomsSatisfied`.
Raw change requires explicit enablement and is converted by the shared
`DeriveForecastCorrection` function. A direct `ForecastCorrection` remains a
calculation input for existing scenarios; explicit `ForecastEnabled: false`
also gates that simulator input.

For example, `--scenario hybrid_winter_30` requests 57 °C at −10 °C and 30% demand
with the 60% cap. `forecast_cooling` requests 59 °C for a −4 °C trend at coefficient
0.5; `forecast_warming_deficit` stays at 57 °C, whereas
`forecast_warming_satisfied` requests 55 °C. `forecast_disabled` stays at 57 °C.

The test firmware delays test startup briefly so the host monitor can attach
after the upload resets the ESP32. The simulator remains available immediately
through its separate serial request protocol.

Both upload commands replace the application on the test ESP32. Close serial
monitors before uploading; reconnect USB if Windows reports a semaphore timeout.
The simulator can remain loaded for subsequent `--json` or interactive sessions.

These tests do not prove physical CAN delivery, radiator response or winter
calibration. HTTP route behavior, validation and persistence require an isolated
controller: CAN read-only, all CAN profiles off, HA discovery disabled, and
unique MQTT topics. Never substitute a fabricated boiler maximum to make an
unconnected test board heat. The [optional HA producer](../assets/Examples/HomeAssistant/README.md)
can be verified with native template evaluation before it is enabled.
